From: Andrea Campanella <emuboy85@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:40:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] No _Float32 for the R5900

GCC gives the R5900 float a format of its own with -march=r5900 and
hard float: no NaN, no infinity, no denormals, the format of the FPU.
_Float32 exists only for an IEEE format, so GCC has none there, and
every header that relies on __HAVE_FLOAT32 fails: "'_Float32' is not
supported on this target". Say so in floatn-common.h. _Float64 and
_Float32x are double, which stays IEEE in software.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [PlayStation 2 port]
---
 bits/floatn-common.h | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/bits/floatn-common.h
+++ b/bits/floatn-common.h
@@ -32,7 +32,13 @@
    floating-point type with the right format for this type, and this
    glibc includes corresponding *fN or *fNx interfaces for it.  */
 #define __HAVE_FLOAT16 0
-#define __HAVE_FLOAT32 1
+#if defined _MIPS_ARCH_R5900 && defined __mips_hard_float
+/* GCC gives the R5900 float a format of its own, without NaN or
+   infinity, and offers no _Float32 for it.  */
+# define __HAVE_FLOAT32 0
+#else
+# define __HAVE_FLOAT32 1
+#endif
 #define __HAVE_FLOAT64 1
 #define __HAVE_FLOAT32X 1
 #define __HAVE_FLOAT128X 0
